---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { projects, thesis } from '../../data/site';
export function getStaticPaths(){ return projects.map(project=>({params:{slug:project.slug},props:{project}})); }
const { project } = Astro.props;
---
<Base title={project.title}>
<section class="page-title narrow"><div class="eyebrow">{project.category}</div><h1>{project.title}</h1><p class="lead">{project.description}</p><div class="tag-row">{project.tags.map(tag=><span class="tag">{tag}</span>)}</div></section>
<article class="prose narrow research-article">
{project.slug === 'multipath' ? <>
  <div class="notice"><strong>Research status.</strong> Prototype measurements are from a specified H200 host and should not be presented as production inference speedups. CUDA integration with vLLM and contention-aware restoration are future evaluation directions.</div>
  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <p>When an LLM is loaded or restored, model weights must move from host memory to GPU HBM. A conventional copy to one target GPU can be bottlenecked by the target's CPU-to-GPU path, even when other GPUs and interconnect links are available. This affects cold starts, model switching and restoration from a suspended state.</p>
  <h2>Core idea</h2>
  <p>Multipath partitions a large transfer into blocks, then distributes blocks across direct and indirect paths. Helper GPUs receive portions from host memory and relay them to the target through GPU peer-to-peer links. The prototype uses asynchronous execution and scheduling across the paths; its performance depends on NUMA placement, interconnect topology and remaining PCIe/NVLink bandwidth.</p>
  <h2>Architecture</h2>
  <div class="diagram" role="img" aria-label="Host memory transfers data directly to a target GPU and indirectly through helper GPUs over peer-to-peer links.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 260" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
      <defs><marker id="arrowMP" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="8" refY="4.5" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L9,4.5 L0,9" fill="none" stroke="#167a5c" stroke-width="1.5"/></marker></defs>
      <rect x="22" y="90" width="155" height="76" rx="12" fill="#e6f2e9" stroke="#7fae95"/><text x="99" y="121" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700">Host memory</text><text x="99" y="143" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12">Blocks / chunks</text>
      <rect x="306" y="14" width="155" height="60" rx="11" fill="#edf5ef" stroke="#7fae95"/><text x="383" y="39" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700">Helper GPU A</text><text x="383" y="57" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12">Host ingress</text>
      <rect x="306" y="184" width="155" height="60" rx="11" fill="#edf5ef" stroke="#7fae95"/><text x="383" y="210" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700">Helper GPU B</text><text x="383" y="229" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12">Host ingress</text>
      <rect x="555" y="90" width="145" height="76" rx="12" fill="#167a5c"/><text x="627" y="122" fill="white" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Target GPU</text><text x="627" y="144" fill="white" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle">HBM</text>
      <g fill="none" stroke="#167a5c" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#arrowMP)"><path d="M177 127 L543 127"/><path d="M177 107 L285 45 L298 45"/><path d="M177 148 L285 214 L298 214"/><path d="M461 45 L550 105"/><path d="M461 214 L550 151"/></g>
      <g font-size="11" fill="#426858"><text x="230" y="116">Direct path</text><text x="486" y="60">P2P</text><text x="490" y="209">P2P</text></g>
    </svg>
    <p class="diagram-caption">Conceptual transfer paths — actual routing is topology- and scheduler-dependent.</p>
  </div>
  <h2>Implementation approach</h2>
  <ol><li><strong>Discover topology:</strong> enumerate visible CUDA devices, peer access and the host NUMA topology.</li><li><strong>Partition:</strong> divide the transfer into independent, bounded blocks and chunks.</li><li><strong>Schedule:</strong> choose the target's direct path or a helper ingress path, balancing locality and observed load.</li><li><strong>Pipeline:</strong> overlap host-to-helper and helper-to-target copies with asynchronous CUDA work and completion tracking.</li><li><strong>Validate:</strong> confirm byte-for-byte correctness and measure throughput across GPU subsets and memory placement policies.</li></ol>
  <h2>Selected H200 microbenchmark evidence</h2>
  <div class="metrics"><div><strong>197.953 GB/s</strong><span>Host → GPU, interleaved placement</span></div><div><strong>189.705 GB/s</strong><span>GPU → host, interleaved placement</span></div><div><strong>~55 GB/s</strong><span>Native single-path reference</span></div></div>
  <p>On an eight-H200 system, the original engine reported approximately <strong>3.576× host-to-device</strong> and <strong>3.442× device-to-host</strong> throughput relative to the native reference under interleaved host-memory placement. These are <em>transfer microbenchmarks</em>, not model-serving TTFT figures.</p>
  <p class="caveat"><strong>Measurement qualification:</strong> the highest labelled eight-GPU host-to-device run used the target and five helpers for nonzero block assignment; GPUs 6 and 7 did not carry blocks. Do not interpret the figure as scaling uniformly across eight active paths. The topology, NUMA policy, test buffer sizes and scheduler revision should accompany any formal publication of this result.</p>
  <h2>Connection to model serving</h2>
  <p>Earlier Ascend-based experiments combined safetensors-aware tensor layout, larger HBM allocations and lazy layer prefetch with multipath movement. The reported qualitative observation was that memory-layout techniques helped steady-state tokens-per-output-token latency (TPOT), while transfer parallelism improved restore/cold-start behaviour. A controlled CUDA/vLLM evaluation is needed before making the same causal claim on H200.</p>
  <p>For related device-memory research, see <a href="/projects/ascend-tpot/">Ascend NPU Memory Optimization ↗</a>, which investigates steady-state decoding (TPOT) rather than model-restoration latency.</p>
  <h2>Next research question</h2>
  <p><strong>Deadline-aware multipath model restoration:</strong> Can a serving runtime restore weights before each layer is needed without disrupting latency for resident requests? Proposed experiments cover cold start, model switching, vLLM sleep/wake, tensor/pipeline parallel layouts, NUMA placement, competing inference workloads and both TTFT and TPOT.</p>
  <h2>Resources and reproducibility</h2><p>The CUDA/H200 benchmark figures above are preliminary research measurements, not a public benchmark release. The original benchmark scripts, topology logs, per-GPU assignments, baseline measurements and source repository have not been published here. They will be linked only after permission and reproducibility review.</p>

</> : project.slug === 'ascend-tpot' ? <>
  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <p>Autoregressive LLM decoding repeatedly reads model weights and KV-cache data from accelerator memory. Even when the weights already reside in NPU high-bandwidth memory (HBM), the organization of tensor allocations and their underlying memory mappings can affect address-translation efficiency and memory-access latency. Loading many independently allocated tensors also complicates management of large contiguous memory regions.</p>
  <h2>Core idea</h2>
  <p>Earlier work explored combining <strong>safetensors-aware tensor merging</strong> with <strong>larger, huge-page-backed Ascend HBM allocations</strong> to improve the memory layout presented to the inference runtime, without changing model weights or numerical computation.</p>
  <h2>Memory-layout approach</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Read model metadata:</strong> use safetensors tensor offsets, shapes and dtypes to plan storage placement.</li>
    <li><strong>Group compatible weights:</strong> organize multiple tensors into larger allocation regions while retaining logical tensor boundaries and correct alignment.</li>
    <li><strong>Allocate NPU storage:</strong> investigate Ascend HBM allocation using larger device-memory pages instead of many small independent mappings.</li>
    <li><strong>Reconstruct tensor views:</strong> associate individual logical tensors with their offsets inside the merged storage.</li>
    <li><strong>Serve and measure:</strong> run vLLM-Ascend inference and compare steady-state token-generation latency against the baseline.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="path-steps"><span>Safetensors metadata</span><span>Tensor grouping</span><span>Large HBM allocations</span><span>Tensor views</span><span>NPU decoding</span></div>
  <h2>How it complements multipath restoration</h2>
  <p>Memory-layout optimization targets the <strong>steady-state decode path (TPOT)</strong>. My <a href="/projects/multipath/">Multipath GPU Transfer</a> work targets <strong>weight movement during model load or restoration</strong>, which may reduce cold-start and time-to-first-token (TTFT) latency. The two approaches address different bottlenecks and could be evaluated together without assuming that their benefits necessarily add linearly.</p>
  <h2>Next evaluation</h2>
  <p>A publishable study will include hardware and runtime versions, model and workload configurations, and controlled TPOT/ITL measurements comparing the baseline, tensor merging, huge-page allocation, and their combination.</p>
</> : project.slug === 'nefele' ? <>
  <h2>The problem</h2><p>Traditional orchestration systems manage containers or services as deployment units, but applications often need a simpler abstraction: start processes anywhere in a cluster, communicate with them by stable identity and manage their lifecycles without handcrafting node-level plumbing.</p>
  <h2>Research idea</h2><p>Nefele explores a cluster-wide process runtime that combines placement, process creation, naming, messaging and supervision, while still allowing efficient local execution and access to underlying compute resources.</p>
  <h2>Control path: create and supervise</h2>
  <div class="path-steps"><span>Local application</span><span>UNIX-socket API</span><span>Distributed placement</span><span>Remote launch</span><span>Identity + monitoring</span></div>
  <p>An application submits a spawn request locally. The distributed runtime evaluates candidate placements, starts the process in the chosen execution environment, returns a usable process identity and maintains lifecycle information. Placement is a control-plane decision; it should not occur for every application message.</p>
  <h2>Data path: identify and communicate</h2>
  <div class="path-steps"><span>Sender process</span><span>Name / identity lookup</span><span>Destination mailbox</span><span>Receiver process</span></div>
  <p>Once processes exist, communication follows their resolved identities and messaging endpoints. This keeps normal message transport separate from the control path used for admission, placement and supervision.</p>
  <h2>What is distinctive?</h2><p>The system's contribution is not a new container scheduler alone: it exposes a <strong>process-oriented distributed programming abstraction</strong> over multiple machines and integrates scheduling, naming, communication and monitoring behind that abstraction. The useful comparison is with process runtimes, cluster resource managers and service orchestrators, each of which addresses only part of this surface.</p>
  <h2>Evaluation</h2><p>Relevant evidence includes process launch latency, placement overhead, message transport latency, scale-out behaviour, naming overhead and resilience to node/process failures. <strong>Measured figures have intentionally not been inserted</strong> until they can be reproduced from public papers or archived test results.</p>
  <h2>Research boundary</h2><p>Migration and checkpoint/restore should be described as potential extensions, not implemented capabilities, unless corroborated by the specific Nefele revision being presented.</p>
  <h2>Publication</h2><p><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.07163" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Nefele: Process Orchestration for the Cloud (arXiv:2006.07163) ↗</a>. No source repository link is claimed until verified.</p>
</> : <>
  <div class="notice"><strong>Doctoral thesis:</strong> <em>Resource Allocation for Mobile Edge Clouds</em> · Computing Science, Umeå University, Sweden (2013–2018).</div>
  <h2>The problem</h2><p>Mobile-edge infrastructures must decide where workloads should execute despite heterogeneous resources, changing demand, limited edge capacity and nontrivial communication costs. A placement strategy that reduces latency can also increase infrastructure cost or worsen utilisation.</p>
  <h2>Research contribution</h2><p>This research studied scalable, cost-efficient resource allocation and workload characterisation for mobile edge clouds. It treated placement and capacity planning as coupled systems questions, rather than assuming unlimited edge capacity or homogeneous execution sites.</p>
  <h2>Method</h2><ol><li>Model workload demand, candidate execution locations and resource constraints.</li><li>Design allocation approaches that balance responsiveness with infrastructure utilisation and cost.</li><li>Use workload analysis to assess capacity and placement decisions.</li><li>Evaluate trade-offs through discrete-event simulation and comparative experiments.</li></ol>
  <h2>Evaluation dimensions</h2><p>The relevant outcome measures include end-to-end latency, resource consumption, allocation efficiency, infrastructure cost and scalability as workload volume or network size increases. Specific numerical claims should be copied only from the thesis and first-author papers.</p>
  <h2>Research trajectory</h2><p>The thesis's emphasis on scheduling under resource and performance constraints connects naturally to later work on distributed runtimes and, subsequently, scheduling transfer resources during GPU model restoration. These are related research themes, not a claim that the same implementation was reused.</p>
  <h2>Thesis and publications</h2><p><a href={thesis.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Resource Allocation for Mobile Edge Clouds — Umeå University thesis (2018) ↗</a>. Related peer-reviewed papers are listed on the <a href="/publications/">publications page</a>.</p>
</>}
